# One GPC's IPL, as done from the O6 panel and a DPS keyboard: select the
# computer, point it at a mass memory, put its IPL display on a CRT, load it,
# and take it to RUN.  4gpc-startup.script plays this once per computer --
# the procedure is the same for each, and only these four values change:
#
#   script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
#
#   gpc   the GPC column the O6 controls act on, 1-5
#   crt   the CRT its IPL display goes to, and the one waited on
#   idp   the IDP given the spring-loaded LOAD toggle
#   kb    the keyboard ITEM 1 EXEC is typed on: KB1, KB2 or KB3
#
# Nothing here is a guessed delay: the load waits for the GPCIPL menu's title
# to appear on CRT $crt, and the run waits for this GPC's MODE talkback to
# read RUN.  It leaves the IPL SOURCE switch off, ready for the next computer.
#
# THE TIMEOUTS ARE SAFETY NETS, NOT DELAYS, so they are sized for the SLOWEST
# host we run on rather than the fastest.  A wait ends the moment its condition
# is met, so a large cap costs a fast machine exactly nothing -- but a small one
# ABORTS THE PROCEDURE on a slow one, and the script then carries on issuing
# crew actions to a computer that never came up.  Measured on a 2011 i7-3930K,
# where a GPC runs at about a quarter of real time: the GPCIPL menu took 41 s
# for the first computer and 66 s for the second, and the first computer needed
# 164 s to reach RUN with nothing else competing.  Each later computer is slower
# again, because the ones already running are taking their share of the host.
# The old caps of 150 and 300 fitted the first computer and would have cut off
# the fourth.

+0     gpc $gpc
+1     source MM1
+1     crt $crt
+0.5   idpload $idp
+0.5   ipl
+3     mode STANDBY
# WAIT FOR THE PAGE TO CHANGE HANDS BEFORE BELIEVING WHAT IT SAYS.
#
# 'wait crt N title TEXT' is a STATE test -- crewscript's _poll_screen matches
# whenever the page currently on show CONTAINS the text.  That is fine for the
# first computer to use a CRT and WRONG for every one after it, because three
# computers share CRT2 here: GPC2's GPCIPL MENU is still on the screen when
# GPC3's turn comes, so GPC3's wait was met after 0.0 s, the script typed
# ITEM 1 EXEC into a computer that had not yet put up its menu, the
# menu-selected load never started, and GPC3 sat there until its RUN wait
# expired.  GPC4 then did the same.  That is why four-GPC runs reached only
# one or two computers in RUN -- not a sync failure at all.
#
# 'new-screen' is a TRANSITION test: it baselines the page when the wait
# begins and returns only when a DIFFERENT one arrives.  Waiting for that
# first means the stale page has actually gone before the title is read.
#
# A fixed delay would not have fixed this.  The IDP reload is given a few
# seconds of WALL clock to clear the display, which was plenty of display
# polls on a fast host and about one simulated second here -- the same
# wall-versus-simulated trap as ledger #164.
wait crt $crt new-screen timeout 300
wait crt $crt title GPCIPL timeout 300
+2     keys $kb ITEM 1 EXEC

# loaded: to RUN, and the IPL source back off
wait gpc $gpc mode-tb RUN timeout 900
+1     crt 0
+2     mode RUN
+2     source OFF
